# draft_attack_id|display_name|kind|definition|question|correct_draft_defense_id|distractor_draft_defense_ids|explanation_and_answer_reason|distinct_example|distractor_scope
1|Reflected distributed denial of service|attack|A flood in which requests carrying a forged victim source address cause third-party servers to send their replies to the victim. Large replies can amplify the traffic.|Your network is sending UDP requests with source addresses that do not belong to it. Which source-network control stops those forged requests before they reach reflectors?|1|145,146,52|Validate packet source addresses at the sending network boundary. This removes a prerequisite for spoofed reflection from that network. Here source verification means network ingress/egress anti-spoofing, not checking an email sender. The victim still needs upstream capacity and mitigation against traffic originating elsewhere.|A gaming service receives unsolicited time-server replies from thousands of systems although it sent no requests.|Scrubbing treats arriving floods, traffic analysis diagnoses patterns, and firewall rules without source-validation logic do not establish the claimed origin.
2|Padding-oracle attack|attack|An attacker learns about decrypted data by making adaptive ciphertext queries to a service whose padding errors or response timing reveal information.|A service returns different errors for altered encrypted cookies and reveals whether their padding is valid. Which redesign supplies confidentiality and integrity together?|2|49,93,166|Authenticated encryption checks ciphertext integrity and rejects tampering before revealing decrypted application data. Correct nonce handling, key management and uniform failures remain necessary. Merely changing an error message can leave a timing oracle; replacing the vulnerable construction is the important change.|A legacy encrypted session token can be manipulated because its server distinguishes invalid padding from invalid content.|An unkeyed hash is not a substitute for authenticated encryption; HTTPS enforcement and rotating keys do not repair the vulnerable cookie construction.
3|Chosen-plaintext cryptanalysis|analysis_model|An analysis setting in which an adversary can obtain encryptions of chosen messages and try to learn secrets from the resulting ciphertexts. Sound modern encryption is designed for this exposure.|A reviewed analysis shows that a proprietary cipher leaks its key when outsiders encrypt chosen inputs. Which catalog practice organizes a tested migration away from that broken cipher?|3|50,166,49|A controlled security change can replace the broken construction, migrate keys and data, test interoperability and provide rollback. Change management is supporting work, not a mathematical defense against chosen-plaintext analysis. The replacement must be an appropriate, reviewed cryptographic scheme with correct mode and nonce use.|A researcher submits selected records to an encryption API and demonstrates patterns caused by a defective design.|Longer output, routine key rotation and an unrelated hash do not establish security for the existing cipher. This question deliberately asks about migration governance, not a cryptographic guarantee.
4|Bluetooth bluesnarfing|attack|Unauthorized retrieval of data over Bluetooth, usually by exploiting a vulnerable service or insecure pairing rather than merely detecting the radio.|An older handset must keep Bluetooth available but should stop advertising itself to casual nearby discovery. Which setting reduces discoverability without claiming to fix its vulnerable service?|4|5,45,18|Disabling discoverability reduces easy discovery during routine scanning. It does not make the address secret or block an attacker who already knows it. Patch the handset, restrict profiles and pairing, or disable Bluetooth when it is not needed to address the actual access path.|An obsolete phone exposes an address book through a poorly protected Bluetooth profile.|Turning Bluetooth off violates the requirement to keep approved connections available; firmware updates and application privacy permissions do not select the requested discovery setting.
5|Bluejacking|attack|Sending unsolicited messages through Bluetooth features, historically object-push services. Receiving a message does not by itself prove theft or device compromise.|A museum tablet needs no Bluetooth connections and receives unsolicited nearby messages. Which action removes this particular radio communication path?|5|4,45,18|Turning Bluetooth off prevents that device from participating in Bluetooth exchanges while the radio is disabled. Blocking unsolicited requests and updating software also matter when Bluetooth is required. Bluejacking should not be confused with bluesnarfing or arbitrary remote code execution.|Visitors repeatedly send unwanted contact cards to a discoverable kiosk.|Hiding discovery leaves a radio active; firmware updates and application privacy permissions do not remove the unused communication path.
6|Shared-cloud side-channel leakage|attack|An attempt to infer another workload's information from shared hardware behavior such as cache timing. Co-location alone does not prove a leak.|Sensitive computations leak timing information to another tenant on the same host. Which hosting choice reduces cross-tenant hardware sharing most directly?|6|120,150,166|Dedicated hardware placement can reduce exposure to an untrusted co-tenant sharing the affected resource. Confirm the provider's actual isolation guarantees; a dedicated virtual instance is not necessarily a dedicated physical host. Vulnerable code and other side channels still require mitigation.|A tenant measures cache behavior to infer activity in a neighboring virtual machine.|Role permissions, a network VPN and key rotation do not remove shared-cache co-residency.
7|WEP wireless-key recovery|attack|Recovery of a key or injection capability against obsolete Wired Equivalent Privacy, whose design has weaknesses that cannot be repaired with a longer Wi-Fi password alone.|A warehouse still uses WEP and can replace its access points and clients. Which listed migration removes WEP rather than merely hiding the network?|7|185,95,166|Move to a properly configured supported WPA3 deployment when clients allow it; remove WEP entirely. Compatibility may require an assessed WPA2-AES interim design. WPA3 is not a promise against jamming, compromised clients or every implementation flaw.|A retired scanner forces an entire wireless segment to use WEP despite newer client devices.|Hidden network names, MAC filters and rotating a WEP key do not repair the WEP protocol design.
8|Virtual-machine escape|attack|A guest workload exploits a hypervisor or virtual-device weakness to cross into host or other guest security domains.|An exposed virtual device has a published guest-to-host flaw. Which program of host patching, interface reduction and secure configuration addresses the virtualization boundary?|8|174,175,65|Hypervisor hardening reduces vulnerable management and emulation surfaces and applies relevant fixes. Guest isolation depends on the hypervisor, host and configuration, so guest antivirus alone is insufficient. A hardened host can still have unknown flaws and needs monitoring and recovery planning.|A malicious workload uses a vulnerable emulated controller to execute outside its assigned virtual machine.|Container mandatory policy, resource namespaces and least privilege do not themselves patch a vulnerable virtual-device implementation in a hypervisor.
9|Quantum-enabled cryptographic threat|category|The potential use of sufficiently capable quantum computers to defeat vulnerable public-key algorithms. It is not evidence that current adversaries can instantly break all encryption.|A long-lived archive uses quantum-vulnerable public-key key establishment and must plan for future confidentiality. Which migration family addresses that algorithmic risk?|9|163,166,50|Use standardized post-quantum algorithms and supported protocols appropriate to the operation, with tested implementations and a migration inventory. Symmetric cryptography and hash functions face different effects and are not all broken in the same way. Migration planning must not overstate current quantum capability.|A government archive assesses information that must remain confidential for decades against harvest-now-decrypt-later collection.|Replacing a symmetric algorithm, merely rotating vulnerable public keys or increasing an unrelated output length does not supply post-quantum public-key security.
10|Voice phishing|attack|A deceptive voice conversation that pressures someone to reveal information, authorize a transaction or install software. Caller ID is not proof of identity.|A caller claiming to be IT asks for your sign-in code. Which action checks the claim without using contact details supplied by that caller?|11|12,85,105|End the call and verify through an independently obtained internal number or approved directory. Even an encrypted call can carry a fraudulent request. Never share authentication codes simply because a voice sounds familiar or a displayed number appears correct.|An automated bank call tells a customer to transfer savings into a purported safe account.|A challenge protocol, additional sign-in factor and email-sender check are not the specified independent telephone callback.
11|Pretexting|attack|Manipulation through a fabricated role, story or situation that makes a request seem legitimate. The channel may be voice, email or in-person contact.|A supposed contractor asks for a restricted document. Your organization has a verified identity challenge process. Which control checks the requester before release?|12|42,110,18|Use an established challenge-and-response process that proves access to an approved identity factor or trusted channel. Public biographical questions are weak challenges and can be researched. Verification does not replace checking whether that verified person is authorized to receive the document.|A visitor claims to be an auditor and asks a receptionist for an employee list.|Training, data restrictions and privacy permissions do not perform a fresh identity challenge against the requester.
12|Dumpster diving for information|attack|Recovering sensitive material from discarded paper, storage media or equipment without authorized access to its contents.|Payroll documents are leaving the office as readable paper waste. Which action makes those documents difficult to reconstruct before disposal?|14|13,62,36|Appropriate shredding or secure destruction reduces recoverability of paper records before waste leaves controlled custody. Media need destruction or sanitization appropriate to their technology. Locked bins help custody but do not destroy information after collection.|A discarded printer contains a storage device holding previously scanned contracts.|Handling rules and access permissions do not destroy paper; reinstalling a computer addresses a different disposal medium.
13|Shoulder surfing|attack|Obtaining information by watching a screen, keyboard or other user interaction, directly or through a camera.|A train passenger can read a colleague's screen from the side. Which control narrows the screen's viewing angle?|16|71,73,110|A privacy filter reduces off-axis screen readability. It cannot stop a camera positioned directly behind the user or prevent observation of keyboard entry. Seating, awareness and minimizing sensitive work in public remain relevant. Recorded video can also be reviewed later, so the absence of a person immediately nearby does not eliminate observation risk.|A camera above a service desk records PIN entry despite a secure network connection.|On-screen input, session expiration and limited data access do not narrow the viewing angle of an active display.
14|Spyware|attack|Software that covertly collects activity, personal information or device data without appropriate authorization or meaningful consent.|An endpoint tool identifies a known unauthorized surveillance program. Which listed tool family is intended to detect and remove that software?|19|23,137,36|Anti-spyware controls can identify known surveillance software and suspicious behavior, then quarantine or remove it. Detection depends on visibility, updates and attacker technique. Permissions review limits exposure but does not reliably remove an already privileged infection.|An unauthorized application captures browsing activity and sends it to an external operator.|Ad filtering and execution policy reduce selected exposure paths, while rebuilding replaces system state; none is the requested surveillance-software detection and quarantine tool.
15|Replay of authentication material|attack|Reuse of captured authentication messages or credentials to obtain access. Reusable passwords, challenge responses and session tokens require different replay defenses.|A device protocol accepts the same captured signed login response twice. Which design binds responses to a fresh server challenge?|21|63,101,85|A unique unpredictable challenge, authenticated as part of the response and checked for one-time use, can prevent reuse in that protocol. Merely adding a random field without checking freshness does not work. Static stolen passwords remain reusable unless authentication design changes.|A recorded badge-system authentication exchange opens a door again because freshness is not verified.|Time windows and ordered counters are different freshness designs; MFA alone does not bind this response to a fresh random challenge.
16|Unwanted advertising software|unwanted_software|Software that delivers advertising, sometimes bundled without clear consent or accompanied by tracking. Not every advertising-supported product is malware.|A browser's advertising requests are the unwanted channel, and malicious software has already been ruled out. Which control filters those requests?|23|19,18,121|An ad blocker can filter ad-related network requests and page elements. It does not uninstall every bundled program or establish that a download is safe. Endpoint cleanup and informed installation choices address unwanted applications themselves.|A free utility adds an advertising component that redirects searches after installation.|Spyware inspection, application permissions and broad destination filtering do not provide the specific browser ad-request filtering function asked for.
17|Rootkit|attack|Software or firmware that hides malicious activity by altering privileged operating-system, boot or hardware behavior. It may also provide persistence.|A defender wants an independent alert when protected system files differ from a trusted baseline. Which control supplies that comparison?|24|26,122,61|Integrity monitoring detects unexpected changes against a baseline that the attacker cannot simply rewrite. A rootkit may lie to tools running inside the compromised system, so offline or hardware-supported verification may be needed. Detection does not itself remove the implant.|A kernel module conceals files and network connections from normal administration tools.|Memory inspection, endpoint response and source review do not supply the specifically requested trusted file-state comparison.
18|Fileless or memory-resident malware|attack|Malicious activity that relies heavily on memory and legitimate tools, reducing conventional executable-file artifacts. It can still leave logs, registry entries or disk traces.|A suspicious script injects code into a live process, but no executable file is available. Which inspection method examines the active process contents?|26|24,131,138|Memory inspection can reveal injected code, credentials or execution state unavailable to a file-only scanner. Protected processes, rapid execution and tool visibility limit coverage. Behavioral telemetry and response capability complement it; fileless does not mean forensically invisible.|A script interpreter retrieves code into memory and maintains persistence through a scheduled task.|File integrity comparison, antivirus as a product class and behavior detection do not specify the requested direct inspection of active memory contents.
19|Wireless radio-frequency jamming|attack|Intentional radio interference that degrades communication availability rather than decrypting traffic. Ordinary interference must be distinguished from an attack.|A supported radio system can change among authorized channels to reduce narrow-band interference. Which mechanism provides that channel agility?|28|34,44,95|Frequency hopping may improve resilience when interference covers only some frequencies and both endpoints support the scheme. It cannot guarantee service against wideband or sufficiently strong jamming. Spectrum regulations, detection and alternate communication paths matter.|A control link loses packets near a continuously transmitting interferer on its current channel.|Encryption, WPA3 authentication and MAC filtering do not remove interfering radio energy or provide the specified channel hopping.
20|Environmental disruption of computing|environmental_threat|Loss or manipulation of physical conditions such as cooling, power or humidity that harms computing operations. It can be accidental or deliberate.|A server room is overheating and staff need an early measured warning. Which control detects the physical condition?|30|29,173,172|Calibrated environmental sensors provide measurements and alerts for conditions such as temperature, water and power. Alerts need actionable thresholds and a response plan. They neither repair cooling equipment nor prove malicious intent. Measurements should be compared with equipment operating limits rather than interpreted as proof of sabotage.|A failed cooling valve causes thermal shutdowns across a rack.|Backups preserve data, alarms relay events and doors resist entry; the question asks for measurement of a physical condition.
21|Offline password cracking|attack|Testing candidate passwords against stolen password-verification data on an attacker-controlled system outside the original login service.|A password database was stolen, so online lockouts cannot slow the attacker. Which listed storage practice raises the computational cost of each offline guess?|32|80,81,84|Use a suitable salted, cost-tuned password-hashing function such as an approved memory-hard password KDF. Unique salts defeat reusable precomputation; work factors make guesses expensive. Ordinary fast hashes and composition rules alone are insufficient. The attacker can keep working without contacting the login service.|An intruder tests candidate passwords against a copied user database on a separate workstation.|Account lockouts, CAPTCHA and login telemetry affect online access; they do not meter calculations against a stolen copy.
22|Cloud-resource misconfiguration|weakness|A deployment or permission error that exposes a cloud resource or grants excessive access. It is a weakness category, not a single attack technique.|A storage bucket accidentally permits public reads. Which listed assurance activity can discover and verify that exposure before deployment approval?|33|65,100,62|Configuration audits can detect public policies, excessive permissions and missing safeguards. The actual corrective action is to remove unintended public access and verify effective permissions. Encryption at rest does not help when the service legitimately decrypts data for an anonymous reader.|An access-policy template makes a confidential document store publicly downloadable.|Privilege and access mechanisms can enforce a correction, but the requested activity is finding and verifying the configuration exposure.
23|Bloatware and unnecessary software|unwanted_software|Unneeded preinstalled or bundled applications that consume resources or enlarge the attack surface. Their presence alone does not establish malicious behavior.|An approved inventory identifies unnecessary bundled applications on an otherwise healthy laptop. Which targeted action removes them without reinstalling the whole system?|35|91,137,38|Use supported removal tools or management policies to uninstall unnecessary packages and verify dependencies. Some applications serve required business functions, and removal must be assessed. A clean reinstall may be warranted after compromise but is not the only response to ordinary bloatware.|A fleet image includes trial utilities that add background services nobody uses.|Baseline hardening, execution rules and education do not themselves perform the identified package uninstallation.
24|Brand impersonation|attack|Use of a trusted organization's identity, look or messaging to deceive people. It can occur through sites, ads, messages or phone calls.|Fraudulent sites copy your brand, and the security team needs to discover new imitations promptly. Which ongoing activity supports detection and takedown?|37|103,114,125|Monitoring relevant domains, advertisements and abuse reports can reveal impersonation. Confirm findings before takedown and preserve evidence. Recognition training helps users but does not automatically discover every copied site; brand familiarity is not authentication.|A fake delivery-company website asks customers to pay a redelivery charge.|Mail filtering and domain email policy cover messaging, while browser typo alerts act during navigation rather than discover copied advertisements and sites.
25|Forged authentication request|category|A fabricated or altered authentication-related message intended to be accepted as genuine. The source label is broad, so the protocol and violated checks must be stated.|A service accepts a login assertion without verifying its issuer or signature. Which security check must establish the assertion's authenticity before a session is granted?|40|119,85,78|Verify the assertion using the expected issuer, audience, signature, freshness and applicable protocol rules. Syntactically valid input is not necessarily authenticated. This scenario is about forged authentication evidence, distinct from CSRF or a verified user's authorization to perform an action.|A fabricated identity token claims administrator status because the relying party never verifies its signature.|Claimant proofing, extra sign-in factors and anti-CSRF values do not verify this assertion's issuer, signature and audience.
26|Quid pro quo social engineering|attack|Offering a benefit or service in exchange for information or actions that the requester should not receive.|A stranger offers technical support in exchange for your password. Which immediate action checks the supposed support request independently?|41|127,110,118|Verify the request through an established help-desk channel and refuse credential sharing. A promised benefit does not establish authority. Training helps recognize the exchange, while operational verification addresses this particular request before action. The verifier should record and report the approach so similar requests can be recognized across the organization.|A caller promises a gift card if an employee installs a remote-access utility.|A policy, limited data access and training do not independently confirm the legitimacy of this specific offer.
27|Piggybacking into a restricted area|attack|Entry by an unauthorized person with another person's assistance or apparent permission. Organizations sometimes use the term interchangeably with tailgating.|A visitor is admitted legitimately but must not wander into restricted rooms alone. Which rule keeps a responsible employee with the visitor?|43|129,130,128|An escort policy defines who can sponsor, accompany and account for visitors throughout controlled areas. It requires staff compliance and a safe challenge process. A badge at the main entrance does not automatically authorize every interior area.|A contractor with a visitor pass follows a helpful employee into an engineering lab without approval.|Door credentials, guard presence and entry vestibules do not define continuous authorized accompaniment of a visitor.
28|Attack against WPA-protected Wi-Fi|category|A family of attacks against Wi-Fi authentication, configuration or implementations. WPA/TKIP, WPA2 and WPA3 are distinct technologies with different weaknesses.|A modern access point has a vendor-confirmed implementation flaw, and a tested security update is available. Which response fixes that known implementation defect?|45|95,169,171|Install the applicable tested firmware update and verify its configuration. Protocol migration can improve security but does not replace patching a vulnerable implementation. Strong enterprise authentication or WPA3-SAE also requires appropriate configuration and client support.|A wireless deployment uses a supported protocol but an unpatched access-point implementation remains vulnerable.|MAC policy, protected management frames and client association settings do not apply the vendor's implementation repair.
29|Denial of service|attack|Making a service unavailable by exhausting resources, exploiting a failure or disrupting dependencies. A single source can cause DoS; distribution is not required.|One API client is exhausting an expensive endpoint with excessive requests. Which control caps that client's request rate before the costly work?|46|145,52,146|Per-client, per-identity or resource-aware rate limits can reduce request exhaustion when placed before expensive operations. Attackers may distribute requests or share addresses with legitimate users. Limits need fair handling and sufficient upstream capacity. Place enforcement before the expensive operation rather than after the constrained resource has already been consumed.|A malformed request crashes a single-threaded service even without a large traffic flood.|Upstream scrubbing, packet filtering and traffic analysis do not impose the stated per-client limit before costly API work.
30|On-path interception|attack|An adversary gains a position between communicating parties and may observe, relay or alter their exchanges. It overlaps with the older man-in-the-middle label.|The application control session must verify both peers independently of any network tunnel. Which mechanism explicitly establishes both application identities?|48|150,149,55|Mutual authentication makes each endpoint verify the other's identity, often with certificates or another suitable authenticated protocol. Confidentiality without authentication can leave an active intermediary. Trust anchors, identity binding and key protection are essential.|A compromised local gateway redirects device traffic to a service impersonator.|A network tunnel, server pin or DNS signature does not by itself supply mutual application-peer authentication.
31|Birthday-bound collision search|analysis_model|Using the birthday effect to find any two inputs with the same hash more efficiently than finding a preimage for one fixed digest.|A signature design uses a hash with an output too short for its collision-security target. Which design change increases the generic collision-search work factor?|50|160,166,164|A suitably longer output increases the birthday-bound work factor, roughly to the square root of the output space for an ideal hash. The algorithm must also resist practical cryptanalysis. This is different from guessing a password or finding a message for one specified digest.|A toy 32-bit checksum quickly produces duplicate values for unrelated files.|Message authentication and key-lifecycle measures change other properties; the question asks for adequate collision-resistant output size.
32|Amplified reflection DDoS|attack|A distributed reflected flood that exploits services whose replies are larger than forged requests, multiplying traffic sent to a victim.|Your ISP wants to stop customer hosts from sending forged-source requests that enlist amplifiers. Which established network practice addresses the spoofed source prerequisite?|51|145,47,55|BCP 38 source-address validation rejects packets inconsistent with their legitimate source network. Deployment at sending networks reduces reflection abuse. It does not guarantee that a victim link cannot be flooded by attacks originating elsewhere or by non-spoofed traffic.|Small queries to exposed UDP services cause much larger replies to converge on a victim.|Scrubbing and load balancing act at different points; DNS response authentication does not validate the source address of outgoing packets.
33|ARP poisoning|attack|Forging local Address Resolution Protocol information so peers associate an IP address with an attacker's link-layer address.|A managed switch can compare ARP messages against trusted address bindings. Which feature rejects inconsistent ARP advertisements?|53|55,162,52|Dynamic ARP Inspection checks ARP against trusted bindings, commonly from DHCP snooping plus explicit exceptions. Incorrect trust ports or static-device handling can cause outages. ARP is a local-link problem; secure higher-layer protocols add protection even if local redirection occurs.|A host announces that the default gateway's IP address belongs to the host's own MAC address.|DNS signatures, VLAN boundaries and general flow filtering do not validate local ARP against trusted switch bindings.
34|DNS cache poisoning|attack|Introducing false DNS data into a resolver cache so later clients receive an incorrect answer.|A resolver must verify that signed zone data came from the authorized DNS zone and was not altered. Which mechanism supplies that chain of DNS data authentication?|55|148,124,150|DNSSEC validates signatures and a chain of trust for signed DNS data. Validation must be enabled and the zone must be correctly signed. It does not encrypt DNS queries, protect unsigned data automatically or prove that the destination site is trustworthy.|Clients of a poisoned resolver are directed to a false address for a payment portal.|TLS, registration monitoring and a VPN do not authenticate the signed DNS zone data itself.
35|Operating-system command injection|attack|Untrusted input changes the meaning of an operating-system command executed by an application, rather than remaining a data argument.|A file-processing service needs to accept only documented operation names and fixed-format identifiers. Which listed control rejects everything outside that small allowed set?|58|76,151,152|Use an explicit allowlist and a non-shell library API with separated arguments wherever possible. Shell escaping is context-sensitive and can leave argument-injection risks. An allowlist must validate the actual command structure and cannot rescue arbitrary command concatenation on its own.|A web diagnostic tool passes user text into a shell command and treats special syntax as instructions.|SQL parameters, HTML output encoding and browser script policy address other interpreters rather than an allowed operating-system operation set.
36|Implementation side-channel attack|attack|Inferring secrets from timing, power, cache use or other observable implementation behavior rather than directly breaking the algorithm.|A remote service's secret comparison runs longer when more prefix bytes match. Which implementation property removes that data-dependent timing pattern?|59|166,163,164|Use well-reviewed constant-time primitives and avoid secret-dependent branches or memory accesses in the relevant path. Constant time is a property of the deployed implementation and platform, not a claim that every operation takes identical wall-clock time. Other physical leakage may remain.|A smart-card power trace reveals patterns correlated with secret-key operations.|Rotation, a cipher name and key custody do not remove secret-dependent timing from the comparison implementation.
37|Logic bomb|attack|Malicious code designed to perform a harmful action when a specified event or condition occurs.|A privileged maintainer proposes a scheduled deletion feature tied to their employment status. Which predeployment practice examines the actual code and intent?|61|24,137,138|Independent code review can detect suspicious triggers, hidden destructive paths and misuse of legitimate privileges. Review requires appropriate expertise and separation of duties; it can miss obfuscated or externally introduced behavior. Access controls and trustworthy build pipelines complement it.|A script is set to wipe a shared folder on a specific date unless an undocumented file exists.|Integrity monitoring, execution policy and behavior detection operate on deployed artifacts or activity rather than review the proposed source change.
38|Replay against an IoT command protocol|attack|Reusing a previously valid device message to repeat an action when freshness or one-time acceptance is not enforced.|A smart lock must bind an unlock response to a fresh unpredictable receiver challenge. Which authenticated one-time value provides that challenge?|21|98,20,101|An authenticated one-time nonce or challenge can distinguish a fresh authorized command from a captured message. The receiver must track or verify freshness securely across reconnects as needed. Resource-constrained devices still require careful protocol design; encryption alone is not replay protection.|A recorded actuator instruction is resent after the operator has stopped the machinery.|Badge authentication and login codes concern other exchanges; ordered counters are not a fresh unpredictable receiver challenge.
39|Malicious insider activity|category|Harmful use of an organization's access, knowledge or trust by someone within its authorized population. Insider risk also includes negligence, which is a different intent.|A support employee can read every department's records although the job needs only one queue. Which principle directly reduces the unnecessary reach?|65|33,120,91|Give the employee only the data and actions needed for the assigned work, with time-bounded elevation where appropriate. Least privilege limits opportunity and impact rather than detecting every malicious act. Monitoring, review and a safe reporting process remain important.|A departing employee misuses legitimately assigned bulk-export rights.|Audits inspect, roles organize permissions and hardening is a broader process; least privilege is the specific authority-minimization principle.
40|Process memory injection|attack|Placing code or executable content inside another process to run in that process's context or evade inspection.|A legacy program tries to execute bytes from a memory region marked for data only. Which operating-system mitigation can block that execution?|66|156,157,137|Data Execution Prevention marks appropriate memory pages non-executable and blocks attempts to execute from them. It does not prevent every write or every injection technique; code-reuse and permission-changing paths can evade this barrier. Fixing the underlying bug and using additional mitigations are still necessary.|An attacker writes malicious code into a trusted process and attempts to start a new thread at that address.|Address randomization, stack guards and application allowlisting do not supply the stated non-executable-page enforcement.
41|Exploitable race condition|weakness|A security-sensitive result changes because concurrent operations interleave unexpectedly, including checks that become invalid before use.|Two threads can both approve withdrawal from the same balance before either updates it. Which listed operation property makes the check-and-update indivisible?|69|46,101,73|Perform the related check and state update as one correctly synchronized atomic transaction or operation. Atomicity must cover the whole invariant, not just an individual read. Mutexes are another synchronization method but the question asks for the property of the combined transaction.|A file path is validated and then replaced by an attacker before a privileged program opens it.|Request rates, message numbering and session lifetimes do not make the balance check-and-update an indivisible transaction.
42|Keystroke logging|attack|Recording user input through software, a hardware device or another interception layer. A keylogger may capture passwords but can also steal ordinary sensitive text.|An endpoint has a known software keylogger. Which listed product class can detect and quarantine the implant rather than merely change how keys are entered?|70|85,137,24|Anti-malware tools can identify known keyloggers and suspicious input interception. They require sufficient visibility and may miss kernel-level or novel implants. Hardware inspection is needed for hardware loggers, and credentials exposed before removal must be treated as compromised.|A keyboard cable contains a small recorder that stores everything typed.|MFA and execution rules protect other stages; file-state monitoring is not the requested malware identification and quarantine engine.
43|Web session hijacking|attack|Using another user's valid session token or manipulating session state to take over an authenticated interaction.|A browser sends a session cookie over plain HTTP as well as HTTPS. Which cookie setting prevents that browser from sending it on the HTTP path?|72|79,85,148|The Secure cookie attribute restricts transmission to secure transport. HttpOnly reduces script access and appropriate SameSite settings reduce some cross-site request risks, but these are different protections. Token rotation, session invalidation and XSS prevention remain necessary.|A stolen session token is replayed after the user completed MFA successfully.|Cross-site cookie policy, MFA and the transport protocol are distinct from the cookie's specific Secure transmission attribute.
44|Security hoax|category|A false security warning or fabricated incident claim that induces harmful action. A mistaken warning is not proof of malicious intent.|A viral message instructs staff to delete a system file to stop an alleged virus. Which first step checks whether the claim is genuine?|74|182,103,118|Check the warning against independent trusted advisories or the organization's security team before acting. Preserve suspicious messages for investigation. An incident plan can organize the response, but verification avoids turning the hoax itself into a destructive action.|A fabricated breach announcement causes staff to send credentials to an unapproved support site.|Intelligence, delivery filtering and training can support response but do not establish the truth of this particular warning.
45|SQL injection|attack|Untrusted input is interpreted as SQL syntax because an application fails to separate database instructions from data.|A search endpoint concatenates user text into a database query. Which coding technique binds that text as data rather than SQL structure?|76|57,151,152|Prepared statements with bound parameters keep supplied values separate from query syntax. Structural elements such as table names still require controlled selection. Database least privilege and error handling reduce impact, while a WAF is supplementary rather than a replacement for fixing the query construction.|A reporting form alters the intended database filter because a submitted value becomes SQL syntax.|Interpreter escaping, HTML encoding and browser resource policy do not bind input values separately from SQL query structure.
46|Cross-site request forgery|attack|A site causes a victim's browser to send an unwanted authenticated request to another site that trusts ambient credentials without sufficient request-origin safeguards.|A banking form needs a server-checked unpredictable value bound to the user's session to reject forged cross-site submissions. Which listed defense supplies it?|78|72,85,148|A correctly implemented anti-CSRF token binds the request to the legitimate interaction. Check the value server-side and protect it from leakage; XSS can often bypass this protection. SameSite cookies and origin checks are useful layers, but they do not eliminate every CSRF scenario.|An attacker-controlled page triggers a logged-in browser to change an account's email address.|Cookie transport attributes, sign-in factors and TLS do not supply the stated unpredictable request-bound anti-CSRF value.
47|Online brute-force password guessing|attack|Repeated systematic password guesses against an account or service. Brute-force search can also be performed offline, where login controls do not apply.|A login service must place an account into a temporary locked state after repeated bad guesses. Which listed control implements that state?|80|84,85,88|An appropriately designed lockout or progressive throttling policy can restrict online guessing. Attackers can abuse rigid lockouts to deny service, so recovery and rate limits need care. This scenario is online; no account setting stops guesses computed against stolen hashes.|A remote-access gateway logs sequential password candidates for one user over several minutes.|Failure monitoring, added identity proof and breach intelligence do not implement the specified account lockout state.
48|Dictionary-based password guessing|attack|Trying likely passwords from a wordlist, breach corpus or predictable variations rather than exploring all character combinations.|At password creation, users must be steered away from predictable words and compromised candidates, not merely reuse. Which listed password-selection policy addresses that choice?|82|80,89,87|Interpret the legacy Complex Passwords label as long, unique, blocklisted-against-common-values passwords or passphrases, not mandatory symbol recipes. A wordlist can target online services or offline hashes. Salts prevent reusable precomputation but do not make a weak individual password unguessable.|A password based on a sports team and the current year is found early in a targeted wordlist.|Online attempt restrictions, avoiding cross-service reuse and KDF cost do not reject predictable password choices at creation.
49|Password spraying|attack|Trying a small number of common passwords across many accounts, often slowly enough to evade simple per-account thresholds.|One common password is being tried once against hundreds of staff accounts. Which telemetry control can correlate those failures across accounts?|84|80,83,81|Correlate failures across accounts, sources and time windows, then investigate and respond to the pattern. Per-account lockouts may not trigger during low-and-slow spraying. Phishing-resistant MFA and avoiding common passwords reduce takeover risk, but monitoring itself is detection rather than guaranteed prevention.|One candidate password is tested across an entire department before the attacker changes candidates.|A lockout sees each account separately, a salt applies offline and CAPTCHA alone does not provide cross-account failure correlation.
50|Rainbow-table password recovery|attack|Searching precomputed hash-chain structures for candidate passwords matching vulnerable unsalted hashes. It is a lookup strategy, not mathematical reversal of a hash.|A legacy password database uses unsalted hashes. Which storage change specifically defeats reusing one precomputed table across all users?|86|80,84,89|A unique random salt per password makes each user's hash computation distinct and makes shared precomputed tables impractical. The salt need not be secret. A cost-tuned password KDF is also necessary because an attacker can still compute guesses for an individual salted record.|A legacy application's identical password hashes reveal that several users chose the same password.|Login restrictions, failure telemetry and using different service passwords do not add a distinct salt to each stored verifier.
51|Credential stuffing|attack|Automated attempts to sign in with username/password pairs exposed by another service. It exploits password reuse rather than discovering a new password through guessing.|An unrelated retailer suffers a breach. Which password habit prevents the stolen retailer password from also being your company email password?|89|80,83,85|Use a different strong password for each account, preferably generated and stored by a trustworthy password manager. Unique passwords reduce cross-service reuse but do not protect a password stolen from the very service being accessed. Phishing-resistant MFA adds a separate layer.|Credentials leaked from a forum are tested against streaming, mail and financial accounts.|Lockouts, salts and MFA provide other protections, but none is the specific habit of choosing a different password for each service.
52|Default credential exploitation|weakness|Unauthorized access using a factory-set, shared or predictable credential that remains active after deployment.|A camera fleet still accepts a published factory administrator password. Which provisioning requirement replaces that default before the cameras are put into service?|90|166,84,85|Require unique initial credentials or a secure first-use change, disable unused default accounts and restrict management access. Here Mandatory Changes refers to changing exposed defaults, not arbitrary periodic password rotation. A changed password does not fix vulnerable firmware.|An internet-accessible appliance can be administered with a credential printed in its public setup guide.|A generic later rotation cycle, monitoring and MFA do not ensure the published default is removed before production use.
53|Protocol downgrade attack|attack|Interference that causes peers to use a weaker protocol or configuration than they could otherwise negotiate.|A service still accepts obsolete protocol versions that clients do not need. Which action removes those versions from the available negotiation choices?|92|166,149,148|Disable deprecated versions and weak algorithms using a supported configuration and test required clients. Authentication must protect negotiation where the protocol requires it. Removing a fallback may expose compatibility problems; HSTS addresses browser HTTP-to-HTTPS behavior rather than every cryptographic downgrade.|An attacker interferes with setup so a client falls back to an older, vulnerable mode.|Key rotation, pinning and the TLS protocol label do not remove the server's configured obsolete negotiation choices.
54|Unauthorized wireless access point|category|An access point deployed without organizational authorization. It may be malicious or an employee's convenience device; discovery does not establish intent.|An office needs to identify access points that are absent from its approved inventory and investigate their wired location. Which system supplies wireless detection and policy response?|94|185,45,171|Wireless intrusion prevention and detection can compare observed access points with authorized infrastructure and help locate suspicious devices. Correct inventory and verification are essential. Automated radio disruption has legal and availability implications and is not universally appropriate.|An employee plugs a personal access point into an internal network port.|SSID suppression, firmware maintenance and client auto-join policy do not discover radios absent from an authorized inventory.
55|Lock picking|attack|Manipulating a lock mechanism to open it without the authorized key and without necessarily forcing the door.|A sensitive storeroom needs a lock designed and independently assessed to resist manipulation of its cylinder. Which listed control addresses the lock mechanism itself?|96|173,172,130|Select an appropriately rated high-security lock as part of a secure door assembly and managed key system. Resistance is not immunity; installation, key custody and other entry paths matter. Electronic monitoring may alert on an opening but does not necessarily improve a mechanical cylinder.|A lock is opened by manipulating its internal components, leaving little obvious door damage.|Alarms, reinforced doors and guards provide other physical layers; they do not specify a cylinder's manipulation resistance.
56|RFID credential cloning|attack|Copying identifying data from a radio credential to reproduce its accepted behavior, particularly when a reader trusts a static identifier.|A badge reader accepts a copied static tag number. Which upgraded badge design can authenticate a secret without transmitting a reusable static proof?|98|129,17,95|Choose cryptographically authenticated RFID with challenge-response and protected keys, not simply encrypted storage or a different printed serial number. Relay attacks and compromised readers remain concerns. The catalog label Encrypted RFID needs this narrower authentication interpretation.|A copied access-tag identifier is written to another token that an old reader accepts.|An access system, biometric check or MAC rule is not the requested fresh cryptographic proof performed by the RFID credential.
57|Abuse of a serverless function|category|Exploitation or misconfiguration of event-driven functions, their permissions, dependencies or triggers. Serverless hosting does not remove application security responsibility.|A compromised image-processing function can read every storage bucket. Which control restricts its execution identity to the bucket and operations it actually needs?|100|140,33,135|An appropriately scoped identity policy limits what that function can do and which resources it can access. Apply least privilege through the platform's IAM rules and test effective permissions. Input validation and dependency security still matter; permissions do not repair the compromised code.|An overly permissive event function can retrieve secrets unrelated to its assigned job.|Patching, configuration inspection and network zones do not enforce the function identity's resource-specific authorization policy.
58|Protocol message replay|attack|Reusing a previously valid message to repeat an authenticated operation because the receiver does not establish freshness or uniqueness.|A signed command stream needs to reject a previously accepted message number. Which protocol field, checked with an anti-replay window, supports that decision?|101|63,21,166|Authenticate sequence numbers and track which values have already been accepted for the session. Handle resets and wraparound safely. A number that is not checked or is not integrity-protected gives no replay guarantee. One-time codes are useful in authentication but do not automatically protect arbitrary command streams.|A captured payment instruction is resent and accepted a second time despite a valid signature.|A timestamp, nonce and credential replacement are not the ordered message-number acceptance window specified in this scenario.
59|Phishing|attack|Deceptive communication that induces a person to reveal information, transfer value or run content. Email is common, but phishing is not limited to email.|An email gateway must quarantine known malicious attachments and suspicious links before delivery. Which listed control performs that delivery-path inspection?|103|114,121,107|Email filtering can assess senders, links, attachments and known malicious patterns before mail reaches users. It can miss novel or compromised-sender attacks and needs incident reporting and endpoint defenses. A delivered message is not necessarily trustworthy merely because it passed filters.|A fake document-sharing notice leads to a sign-in page controlled by an attacker.|Domain mail policy, web destination rules and exercises do not perform the specified attachment quarantine before mail delivery.
60|Spear phishing|attack|Phishing tailored to a person or group using their role, relationships or recent activity to make the approach credible.|A project-specific message requests a confidential design. Which immediate check verifies the sender through a trusted route rather than trusting familiar project details?|105|103,114,108|Confirm the request and identity through an independently known channel before releasing information. A real compromised mailbox can still pass email authentication, so verification should include intent and authorization. Tailoring is the distinguishing feature, not proof of legitimacy.|An attacker mentions a current procurement meeting to persuade its participants to open a malicious attachment.|Filtering, domain authentication and executive training do not independently confirm the sender's intent for this disclosure.
61|Executive-targeted phishing|attack|Targeted phishing aimed at senior or otherwise high-value decision makers, often exploiting authority, confidential projects or payment powers.|A purported chief executive urgently requests a changed payment destination. Which established process checks the request through a known channel before payment?|109|114,103,85|An out-of-band verification protocol and appropriate approvals can interrupt executive impersonation even when the message appears authentic. No employee should be exempt because a request invokes seniority. Training prepares staff and access controls limit exposure but are not transaction verification.|A tailored message uses a board member's name and a confidential acquisition as a pretext for a transfer.|Domain policy, mail inspection and MFA do not verify the legitimacy of the changed bank account for this transaction.
62|SMS phishing|attack|Deceptive text messages that lure recipients into a fraudulent site, call or software installation. Sender labels and message threads can be misleading.|A text says your account is locked and supplies a recovery link. Which user action avoids trusting that message's destination?|111|104,121,85|Open the organization's application or enter a previously known address independently rather than using an unexpected text link. This reduces exposure to the particular lure but does not guarantee the genuine service or device is uncompromised. Verify urgent claims through trusted support.|A false parcel notification asks for a small card payment through a texted link.|Training, destination filtering and MFA are supporting safeguards; the requested immediate user action is independent navigation.
63|Business email compromise|attack|Fraud using impersonated or compromised business communication to manipulate payments or release information, often without malware.|A supplier requests new bank details by email. Which control requires independent approval and account-change checks before money moves?|115|103,85,105|Use segregation of duties, verified account-change procedures and transaction approval thresholds. These controls address the payment process even when a genuine mailbox has been compromised. Email authentication and MFA reduce some entry paths but do not certify that a payment instruction is legitimate.|A criminal takes over a supplier mailbox and replaces an invoice's destination account.|Mail inspection and identity checks do not impose separation of duties or account-change approval on the payment itself.
64|False or misleading information|category|Inaccurate information that may be spread accidentally or deliberately. Deliberate deception is often called disinformation; falsity alone does not prove intent.|A widely shared breach claim has no linked evidence. Which analytical step checks the underlying facts against independent reliable material?|116|37,124,182|Verify primary evidence, dates, context and independent corroboration before repeating the claim. A source's popularity does not establish accuracy, and uncertainty should remain visible. Content moderation is a policy action, not a substitute for verifying whether a claim is true.|An old outage screenshot is circulated as proof of a new compromise.|Observation, domain discovery and threat information do not replace checking this claim's primary evidence and context.
65|Identity impersonation|attack|Pretending to be a trusted person or entity to gain access, information or action. It may be digital or physical.|A person requests a replacement employee credential. Which procedure checks that the claimant is actually the employee before an account is recovered?|119|40,85,105|Use identity verification proportionate to recovery risk, with evidence and independent channels that an attacker cannot easily supply. A verified identity still needs authorization for the requested operation. Avoid making public biographical information the decisive proof.|A visitor borrows an employee's name and job title to request internal access.|Token validation, a returning user's authentication factors and email identity differ from proofing the claimant during recovery.
66|Watering-hole compromise|attack|Compromising a site or service frequented by an intended victim group so visits expose them to malicious content or credential theft.|A frequently used trade site is compromised and is now known to deliver malicious content. Which control can block access to that destination pending remediation?|121|103,124,125|A web filter can deny known harmful destinations or content categories and reduce exposure while the site is investigated. Encrypted traffic visibility, false positives and new sites limit coverage. Browser patching and endpoint protections remain necessary when a site has not yet been classified.|An engineering community portal serves a malicious script only to visitors from selected organizations.|Mail inspection, registration discovery and typo alerts do not block a known compromised but correctly spelled web destination.
67|Typosquatting|attack|Registering or using a domain resembling a legitimate name through spelling mistakes or similar characters to attract or deceive users.|Your security team wants early notice of newly registered look-alike domains. Which control watches registrations rather than relying only on user reports?|124|114,103,121|Domain monitoring can find suspicious registrations and support investigation, warnings and takedown. Similar spelling alone is not proof of abuse. It does not stop someone from visiting a malicious site before detection. Use the finding to investigate actual behavior; unrelated legitimate owners can also hold similar names.|A fake vendor address differs from the genuine domain by one missing letter.|Mail-domain policy and mail or browsing filters do not provide the requested look-alike registration discovery.
68|Baiting with removable media|attack|Offering something attractive, such as a labeled USB drive, to induce unsafe handling that exposes a device or information.|Unapproved USB storage is being left near an office entrance. Which endpoint policy can prevent those storage devices from mounting on managed workstations?|126|137,91,133|Device-control policy can block unapproved USB storage or require explicitly authorized devices. Distinguish storage from other USB device classes and preserve legitimate operational needs. Scanning alone may miss unknown malware or a device that emulates a keyboard.|A drive labeled Salary Review tempts employees to connect it to a work computer.|Execution policy, baseline configuration and safer browsing do not specify device-class rules preventing a USB storage mount.
69|Tailgating into a controlled area|attack|Following an authorized person through an access boundary without completing required individual entry checks.|A laboratory needs a controlled vestibule that checks one person's authorization before the next door opens. Which physical control supplies it?|128|43,97,173|A correctly designed access-control vestibule can enforce one-at-a-time entry and reduce unnoticed following. Emergency egress, accessibility and safe operating procedures must be designed in. The terms tailgating and piggybacking vary; the learning scenario states the actual behavior.|An unauthorized person carries a box and follows staff through a badge-controlled door.|Escorting, electronic access and alarms are distinct from a controlled one-person entry vestibule.
70|Computer virus|attack|Malware that attaches to a host file or other executable content and replicates when that host executes. This differs from autonomous network propagation by a worm.|A known file-infecting malware family has been identified in downloaded programs. Which listed tool detects and quarantines recognized infected files?|131|137,135,65|Antivirus combines signatures and other detection methods to find malicious files and activity. Keep it updated and verify remediation; new or concealed malware can evade detection. Recovery may require clean rebuilding and restoration rather than trusting a single successful scan.|An infected program modifies other executable files when users run it.|Execution allowlists, network zones and reduced privileges restrict opportunity or impact rather than identify and quarantine infected files.
71|Network worm|attack|Malware that propagates autonomously between systems, commonly by exploiting exposed services or reusable access paths.|A worm is spreading between departments over services they do not need to share. Which architectural control restricts that lateral traffic?|135|131,132,65|Segmentation limits allowed communication between defined zones and can contain spread when enforced by tested access rules. It does not remove an already infected host or protect an explicitly permitted vulnerable service. Patching and incident containment remain necessary.|An unpatched service allows infected hosts to compromise reachable peers without a user opening a file.|Malware detection, updates and privilege reduction do not define the requested network-zone architecture.
72|Trojan horse malware|attack|Malicious software presented as useful or legitimate so a user or administrator runs it. Disguise, rather than self-replication, defines this category.|A managed workstation must run only explicitly approved applications, even if a user downloads a convincing fake utility. Which execution policy addresses that requirement?|137|179,22,123|Application allowlisting restricts executable content to approved identities, rules or trusted publishers. Rule design and update processes matter, and approved tools can still be abused. Signing alone does not establish that a publisher's program is safe.|A fake video-conferencing installer also opens a covert remote-control channel.|Signatures, trusted distribution and scanning inform trust decisions but do not enforce the stated approved-executable policy.
73|Ransomware and extortion|attack|Malware or an intrusion campaign that denies access to data or systems and demands payment, sometimes also stealing data for extortion.|After ransomware, a team needs to restore data without paying and without trusting copies writable by the attacker. Which recovery control is essential?|29|143,135,131|Maintain tested, isolated or immutable backups with protected credentials and clean recovery procedures. Restoration does not undo exfiltration, eliminate persistence or guarantee a fast recovery. Containment and investigation must establish that restored systems will not be immediately compromised again.|A criminal encrypts a file server and threatens to publish previously stolen documents.|Containment, zone boundaries and malware detection do not create an independent recoverable data copy.
74|Remote access Trojan|attack|Malware providing covert or unauthorized remote control of a device. Legitimate remote administration tools can also be abused, but are not automatically Trojans.|A workstation begins an unusual external control session through an allowed connection. Which listed monitoring method flags behavior that differs from its established baseline?|142|73,137,65|Anomaly detection can reveal new destinations, unusual remote activity or unexpected process behavior and trigger investigation. Baselines drift and legitimate changes can look suspicious. MFA does not prevent malware already executing from using the victim's session or making outbound connections.|A disguised utility gives an attacker interactive access to files, microphone and running applications.|Session expiry, execution rules and privilege limits do not compare the connection pattern with an established behavioral baseline.
75|Botnet activity|attack|Coordinated activity by a collection of compromised or otherwise abusively controlled devices. Individual devices may send spam, floods or other commands.|A known botnet control domain must be redirected to an authorized defensive destination to interrupt command contact and observe affected clients. Which technique does that?|144|52,55,146|Authorized DNS or infrastructure sinkholing can divert control traffic and help identify compromised clients. It depends on the malware's command scheme and the defender's legal and technical authority. Devices still require remediation; blocking one domain does not remove the bot.|Compromised cameras periodically contact a shared command service before sending attack traffic.|Flow restrictions, DNS data authentication and traffic analysis do not implement the authorized command-domain redirection.
76|Distributed denial of service|attack|A coordinated availability attack using multiple sources. Sources need not be infected hosts, and a high request count alone does not prove malicious intent.|A distributed flood will saturate your internet circuit before local filtering can act. Which listed service removes harmful traffic upstream before forwarding legitimate traffic?|145|47,52,135|An adequately provisioned upstream scrubbing service can absorb and filter attack traffic before the constrained link. Routing arrangements, application-layer attacks and false positives need planning. Local server capacity or an on-premises firewall cannot create missing upstream bandwidth.|Thousands of independent sources exhaust a public service's network and request-processing capacity.|Balancing backend work, local packet rules and network zoning do not provide upstream flood absorption before the constrained circuit.
77|Man-in-the-middle interception|attack|An older term for active intermediary attacks in which an adversary relays or alters communication between parties. It substantially overlaps on-path attacks.|A browser must authenticate a site's server and protect the session against local interception. Which protocol, with correct certificate validation, supplies that protection?|148|55,48,92|Properly configured TLS authenticates the server and protects data confidentiality and integrity in transit. Validate the intended hostname, certificate chain and protocol configuration; do not bypass certificate errors. Compromised endpoints and trusted malicious certificates remain outside a simple transport guarantee.|A hostile hotspot attempts to substitute its own certificate for a banking website.|DNS authentication, mutual identity proof as a property and removal of obsolete options do not name the requested protected web-session protocol.
78|Cross-site scripting|attack|Untrusted content executes as script in a site's browser security context, potentially reading data or acting with the user's privileges.|A page displays an untrusted comment as plain text inside HTML. Which context-specific output treatment prevents it becoming HTML markup or script?|151|76,57,39|Use the appropriate HTML text-context encoding at the output boundary and safe rendering APIs. Different contexts need different handling; some should never accept untrusted interpolation. CSP is defense in depth, and sanitization is needed when intentionally allowing a restricted subset of HTML.|A stored comment executes code for every administrator who views it because it is inserted as markup.|SQL binding, shell escaping and input checking do not encode untrusted plain text for its HTML output context.
79|Path traversal|attack|Manipulating file-path input to access data outside an intended directory or resource scope.|A download service accepts a filename and must reject paths that resolve outside an approved root. Which operation supports checking the resolved path rather than the raw spelling?|154|62,39,151|Canonicalize and validate the resolved path against an authorized root, with platform-specific separators and encoding handled correctly. Prefer safe file APIs and explicit identifiers. Symbolic links and check/use races can defeat a naive string-prefix test even after normalization.|A crafted filename makes a document server read an operating-system file instead of a user's export.|Permissions, general input checks and HTML encoding do not specify canonical file-path resolution and confinement.
80|Buffer overflow|weakness|Writing outside an allocated memory buffer, potentially corrupting data or control flow. Read overflows are a separate confidentiality issue.|A program copies input into a fixed-size array without checking length. Which engineering practice addresses the root defect instead of merely making exploitation harder?|67|66,137,24|Use memory-safe abstractions or languages, bounds-checked APIs and validation appropriate to the object size. Test boundary cases and review unsafe interfaces. ASLR, stack canaries and DEP are complementary mitigations rather than proof that the overflow no longer exists.|An oversized network message overwrites memory beyond a parser's receive buffer.|Non-executable pages, execution policy and integrity alerts do not repair the unchecked out-of-bounds write.
81|Privilege escalation|attack|Obtaining permissions or execution authority beyond the actor's authorized level, through flaws, misconfiguration or abuse of trusted functions.|An application routinely runs as administrator despite needing only one data directory. Which principle reduces the privileges available if it is compromised?|65|120,91,174|Run with only required rights, separating administrative operations and limiting elevation duration. Least privilege reduces impact and some escalation paths but does not patch a kernel flaw or prove that all authorization checks are correct. Monitor and review privileged activity.|A local service flaw turns a low-privilege account into an administrative process.|Role administration, baseline hardening and a mandatory policy engine are implementations or processes, not the requested least-authority principle.
82|Pass-the-hash authentication abuse|attack|Using stolen NTLM password-hash material to authenticate without recovering the plaintext password. It differs from offline cracking and ordinary password replay.|An assessed Windows deployment can retire NTLM without breaking required services. Which listed change removes that specific authentication path for stolen NTLM hashes?|158|80,87,83|Restrict or disable NTLM where supported after compatibility assessment, and combine this with credential isolation, unique local administrator secrets and least privilege. Kerberos use alone does not eliminate credential theft or all NTLM fallback paths. Ticket and key abuse remain possible in other protocols.|A stolen administrator NTLM hash is used to authenticate laterally to another host.|Online restrictions, password-guess cost and salts do not remove an accepted NTLM authentication path for stolen hash material.
83|Hash collision exploitation|attack|Finding or exploiting two different messages with the same hash value to undermine a use that assumes collision resistance. This does not necessarily find a preimage for a chosen digest.|A document-signing workflow relies on a hash with practical collision attacks. Which policy requires replacing that broken primitive with a suitable current one?|161|166,50,164|Use a cryptographic primitive whose security properties fit the workflow and whose collision resistance remains supported. Migration includes protocol and interoperability review. HMAC can authenticate messages with a shared secret but is not a drop-in replacement for a public digital-signature design.|Two specially constructed documents share a digest under a broken hash and undermine a signature workflow.|Key rotation, a longer output and key custody do not establish that a collision-broken primitive has been replaced safely.
84|Packet capture and sniffing|potentially_legitimate_activity|Collecting network packets for analysis. It can support authorized troubleshooting or unauthorized surveillance; intent and permission determine the context.|An analyst must protect application contents from an unauthorized passive observer on the network. Which control makes those contents unreadable in transit when implemented correctly?|34|170,52,53|Authenticated encryption protects content against observers and active alteration on the protected path. Traffic timing, addresses and sizes may remain visible. Encryption must terminate at trusted endpoints; authorized capture is still useful for troubleshooting without declaring every observer malicious.|A network administrator captures packets under an approved incident-response procedure while an attacker captures them without permission.|Detection, flow policy and ARP inspection do not make captured application contents cryptographically unreadable.
85|Known-plaintext cryptanalysis|analysis_model|Analysis using plaintext/ciphertext pairs already known to the adversary to investigate an encryption system. Secure modern designs anticipate such exposure.|A proprietary encryption scheme fails when predictable headers are known. Which established symmetric primitive can underpin a reviewed replacement instead of inventing another cipher?|163|49,166,2|AES is a standardized block cipher that must be used in an appropriate authenticated mode with sound key and nonce handling. Merely naming AES is not a complete system design. Known plaintext is a normal security assumption, not automatic evidence that every cipher is broken.|Predictable file-format headers expose known message portions to an analyst studying a weak cipher.|A hash, key replacement and an authenticated-encryption construction are not the specific standardized symmetric block cipher requested.
86|Ciphertext-only cryptanalysis|analysis_model|Studying encrypted messages without known plaintext to infer information or attack a weak scheme. Even secure encryption can expose metadata such as length.|A developer proposes an unreviewed cipher because intercepted messages appear unreadable. Which engineering rule calls for well-reviewed standards and correct implementations instead?|165|50,164,163|Choose established cryptographic constructions with security properties suited to the application, then test implementation and key use. Lack of readable text is not evidence of cryptographic strength. Randomness, nonce uniqueness and authenticated encryption matter alongside standards selection.|A repeated-pattern homegrown cipher reveals structure across many intercepted encrypted records.|Output length, key custody and one named primitive do not establish the complete standards-based design and implementation policy.
87|Evil-twin wireless network|attack|A deceptive access point imitates a trusted network identity to attract client connections, steal authentication material or intercept traffic.|A managed Wi-Fi client must reject an enterprise authentication server whose identity is not trusted for that network. Which trust service supports validating the server certificate?|167|150,94,171|Configure the client to validate the expected authentication-server certificate and name using the appropriate CA trust policy. Possession of any certificate is not sufficient. A VPN can protect traffic after a trusted tunnel forms but cannot make a fraudulent Wi-Fi login page safe.|A hotspot copies a company's network name and asks users to enter their enterprise password into a fake sign-in flow.|A VPN, wireless detection and auto-join policy do not supply the expected authentication-server certificate trust decision.
88|Forged Wi-Fi deauthentication|attack|Sending forged management messages to disconnect wireless clients when the relevant management frames lack effective protection.|Compatible wireless infrastructure and clients must reject forged protected management messages. Which Wi-Fi mechanism addresses that frame-authentication problem?|169|95,45,171|Protected Management Frames, introduced through 802.11w, protect specified robust management frames when negotiated and enforced. They do not protect every management frame or defeat radio jamming. Verify client compatibility and actual configuration rather than assuming the label covers all wireless denial of service.|A nearby sender repeatedly disconnects clients from an older unprotected wireless network.|MAC rules, software updates and auto-join restrictions do not name the management-frame authentication feature.
89|KARMA-style wireless impersonation|attack|An access point responds deceptively to clients searching for previously used networks, exploiting unsafe automatic association behavior.|Managed devices should not automatically join networks merely because a nearby responder claims a remembered name. Which client policy addresses that initial association behavior?|171|95,185,94|Disable unsafe automatic connection and remove obsolete network profiles, using authenticated network configuration where possible. A user can still approve a deceptive network manually. A VPN reduces some traffic exposure only after a trusted tunnel is established, not the false association itself.|A laptop probing for an old guest network associates with an attacker's responding access point.|MAC rules, hidden-name presentation and wireless detection do not change unsafe client automatic-association behavior.
90|Forced physical entry|attack|Breaking or forcing a physical access boundary rather than opening it with an authorized credential or manipulating its lock mechanism.|A facility needs greater resistance to a door being kicked open. Which listed measure strengthens that physical barrier?|172|130,96,97|Use an appropriately rated door, frame, hinges and anchoring as an integrated assembly. A stronger panel with a weak frame is not sufficient. Entry resistance must be balanced with fire egress and accessibility; it delays attackers rather than guaranteeing prevention.|An intruder forces a poorly anchored service door despite its undamaged lock cylinder.|Human response, a pick-resistant cylinder and electronic access do not strengthen the whole door and frame against force.
91|Container breakout|attack|Crossing a container's intended isolation boundary to affect the host or other workloads through vulnerabilities, excessive privileges or exposed interfaces.|A Linux host needs mandatory rules limiting what a containerized process can access even when ordinary file permissions allow it. Which mechanism provides that policy enforcement?|174|180,65,156|SELinux can enforce mandatory access rules based on labels and policy, limiting resources a process can use. Its effectiveness depends on enforcing mode and appropriate profiles. Namespaces alone are not a complete security boundary, and a privileged container or vulnerable kernel can undermine isolation.|A container with an exposed host management socket gains control outside its intended workload scope.|Admission rules, least privilege and memory randomization do not provide the specified label-based mandatory runtime access engine.
92|IoT devices used in DDoS|attack|Compromised internet-connected devices participate in a distributed availability attack. The device category describes sources, not a special kind of traffic immunity.|A camera fleet exposes a vendor-fixed remote compromise flaw. Which action removes that known weakness from the devices when a safe update is available?|132|91,145,134|Apply supported, verified device updates and review management access and default credentials. Test operational compatibility and replace unsupported devices when needed. Updates do not disinfect every compromised device or absorb a flood already in progress.|Compromised cameras are instructed to send coordinated traffic against an external service.|Configuration hardening, flood scrubbing and network access rules do not apply the vendor's code fix.
93|Attack against operational technology|category|Malicious interference with systems that monitor or control physical processes. Safety, availability and engineering constraints shape the response.|An industrial zone needs tightly controlled communication between engineering systems and plant controllers. Which listed boundary control can enforce allowed industrial traffic?|177|173,135,132|An appropriately designed industrial firewall can restrict approved paths and, where supported, inspect relevant protocols. Coordinate policy changes with operators and safety engineers; blocking a legitimate command can be hazardous. Firewalls do not replace host hardening, recovery planning or physical safety controls.|An attacker changes a process setpoint through an exposed engineering workstation.|Physical alarms, general network zoning and software updates do not provide the specified industrial protocol boundary inspection.
94|Software supply-chain compromise|attack|Compromise of a supplier, dependency, build system or delivery channel that reaches users through trusted relationships or software distribution.|A release process needs evidence that downloaded packages came from the approved publisher and were not modified after signing. Which mechanism supplies that check?|179|123,164,137|Verify signatures against a trusted publisher identity and protect the signing process. A valid signature demonstrates integrity relative to the signed content, not that the publisher's code is benign. Compromised build or signing systems can produce legitimately signed malicious releases.|A dependency update introduces attacker code into many downstream products.|Scanning, key management and execution policy do not themselves verify this release package against its publisher signature.
95|Kubernetes workload or cluster compromise|category|Abuse of container workloads, cluster permissions, exposed management interfaces or insecure deployment settings in Kubernetes.|A cluster should reject new workloads requesting privileged containers or unsafe host access. Which listed admission-policy family can enforce those workload restrictions?|180|175,174,65|Use supported admission controls such as Pod Security Admission and suitable policy enforcement. The historical PodSecurityPolicy resource was removed; do not recommend deploying that removed API. RBAC, network rules, runtime security and patching address additional cluster risks.|A workload manifest requests excessive host privileges and can inspect sensitive host resources.|Resource views, mandatory runtime policy and least privilege do not perform the workload-admission check described.
96|Exploitation of a zero-day vulnerability|attack|Exploitation of a vulnerability before an effective fix or sufficient defender knowledge is available. An unknown flaw is not automatically being exploited.|No signature exists for a new exploit, but an endpoint can flag suspicious patterns such as unexpected process creation. Which detection approach uses those behavior-based clues?|181|140,143,135|Heuristic detection can identify suspicious patterns without an exact known signature. It produces false positives and can miss new techniques; it does not guarantee detection of every zero-day. Isolation, least privilege and rapid investigation reduce impact while a fix is developed.|An attacker uses an unpatched browser flaw before the vendor publishes remediation.|A patch, containment action and network architecture do not detect an unfamiliar behavior pattern without a known signature.
97|DNS infrastructure abuse|category|A broad group of attacks against name resolution or its administration, including poisoning, hijacking, flooding and unauthorized changes. They require different defenses.|A resolver must authenticate signed DNS answers, and the team is selecting what the broad Secure DNS label means for this case. Which listed defense family should be configured specifically for DNSSEC validation?|183|146,124,150|Define the DNS control explicitly: DNSSEC validates signed data, while encrypted DNS transports protect a particular resolver connection. Registrar security, resilient hosting and monitoring address other DNS threats. A vague Secure DNS checkbox must not stand in for a complete, threat-specific design.|An attacker changes a registrar account's delegation settings despite the organization's encrypted client-to-resolver traffic.|Traffic analysis, look-alike domain monitoring and a VPN do not themselves validate a DNSSEC signature chain.
98|Browser-resident session manipulation|attack|Malicious code inside a browser or its extensions alters transactions or steals session data after transport decryption. It is distinct from a network intermediary.|A browser is suspected of modifying transactions locally. Which immediate containment action separates that affected endpoint from production access while a clean device is used?|143|148,85,137|Isolate the compromised browser or endpoint from sensitive sessions, invalidate exposed sessions and investigate from a trusted environment. TLS cannot protect data from malicious code running at its trusted endpoint. Merely adding a browser extension is not a reliable cleanup method.|A malicious extension changes payment details within the genuine bank page after the TLS session is established.|Transport protection, added sign-in proof and execution policy do not contain an already compromised browser session.
99|Wireless network surveying (wardriving)|potentially_legitimate_activity|Discovering and mapping wireless networks from a moving location. It may be authorized research or surveying; mapping alone is not an intrusion.|An authorized survey can still observe your network name. Which control protects the content of wireless communications instead of trying to conceal that public radio identifier?|34|95,171,28|Use properly authenticated modern wireless encryption to protect transmitted content. Encryption does not prevent detection of radio transmissions, network names or all metadata. Surveying must follow authorization and privacy rules; do not teach that wardriving inherently proves malicious intent.|A contracted wireless assessor maps coverage and records access points with the owner's permission.|MAC filtering regulates associations, disabling auto-connect changes client behavior and frequency hopping is not a substitute for authenticated content encryption.
100|Malicious software update|attack|An update distributes unwanted or malicious behavior through a software delivery path, whether by tampering or compromise of a trusted publisher.|A package mirror may have been tampered with. Which check verifies the update against an independently trusted publisher signature before installation?|179|178,140,164|Verify publisher signatures and release provenance using a trust route independent of the suspect mirror. Signing does not prove benign content if the publisher, build pipeline or signing key was compromised. Staged rollout, review and recovery plans limit the consequences of a bad update.|An attacker replaces an unsigned update on a distribution server with a modified package.|Supplier assessment, applying patches and managing keys do not themselves perform the requested publisher-signature verification on this downloaded package.
